# Patterns to ignore when building packages.
# Matched against the relative path, supports shell glob, one pattern per line.

.DS_Store
.git/
.gitignore
*.swp
*.bak
*.tmp
*.orig
*~

# EVERY DIRECTORY PATTERN BELOW IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND THAT IS
# LOAD-BEARING. .helmignore is applied when Helm LOADS a chart directory, not
# only when it packages one, so an over-broad pattern removes files from every
# `helm template`, `helm lint` and `helm package` at once - and `helm lint
# --strict` cannot see it happen. An unanchored `hack/` matches at ANY depth:
# gd-p0-rev-2 measured it swallowing `templates/hack/nested.txt`. `tests/` is
# the dangerous one to leave bare, because `templates/tests/` is where Helm's
# own test hooks conventionally live, so the bare form would silently drop a
# future test hook and the render would simply be smaller.
#
# tests/chart-integrity.sh asserts each of these exclusions by name, and
# asserts the surviving file list by name too - a negative here is only
# meaningful beside the positives that prove the listing was non-empty.

# CI fixture values. These exist to be passed to helm lint and helm template
# with -f; they are not defaults and must never be mistaken for them, least of
# all inside a packaged chart where image.repository points at an example
# registry path that does not exist.
/ci/

# The assertion harness. Excluded on evidence rather than taste: helm package
# does not preserve the executable bit (verified - the scripts arrive 0644 in the
# tarball), so the shipped copy runs only under an explicit `bash x.sh` and the
# obvious `./x.sh` fails with permission denied. A half-working test suite inside
# a released artifact is worse than none. The scripts also assert the behaviour of
# a source tree, and Phase 6 will wire them to CI, which runs on the repository.
#
# This cuts the opposite way from VALIDATION.md below, on purpose: that file is
# most useful to whoever holds the package, and these are most useful to whoever
# holds the repository.
/tests/

# The golden renders. They are the expected output of this chart, so shipping
# them inside the chart puts a stale copy of the answer next to the thing it is
# the answer to. They are also large and of no use to whoever holds the package.
/golden/

# hack/verify.sh, excluded for the same reason as tests/ above: helm package
# does not preserve the executable bit, and it asserts against a source tree.
/hack/

# VALIDATION.md is deliberately NOT ignored. It is the record of which
# acceptance criteria have never been run, and it is most useful to exactly the
# person who has a packaged chart and a real cluster.
